# SakkirinaSolo.cs -> SakkirinaScaled.cs  (time-budget-scalable baseline)
#
# WHY THIS IS A PATCH AND NOT A SOURCE FILE
# SakkirinaScaled is ~1190 lines, of which ~35 differ from SakkirinaSolo, the
# 2025 competition winner. Shipping it as a source file would amount to
# redistributing someone else's competition entry under a different class name.
# Same reasoning as scripts/sakkirina_gen.patch and scripts/sakkirina_half.patch:
# the patch carries only our changes, and scripts/fetch_baselines.sh applies it
# to the author's own file, fetched from the competition archive.
#
# SakkirinaSolo is the work of its author and is NOT covered by this
# repository's licence. See README.md, "Third-party agents".
#
# WHAT THIS AGENT IS FOR
# experiments/configs/time_scaling.json asks how the DeepSets agent's advantage
# behaves as the per-turn time budget moves from 2s to 30s. That needs a
# baseline whose budget moves WITH it -- holding the baseline at 9.8s while the
# treatment varies would measure the gap between the two budgets, not the effect
# of the budget. SakkirinaSolo's budget is two hardcoded literals, so this
# derives a copy whose budget is an environment variable.
#
# It is also how the stock baseline's evaluations-per-turn gets measured at all:
# stock SakkirinaSolo emits no throughput telemetry, and at
# SOT_BASELINE_TIME_SCALE=1.0 this agent is timing-identical to it. That figure
# is what experiments/configs/equal_effort.json matches against -- see
# tools/benchmark_cluster.py --calibrate.
#
# WHAT THE PATCH CHANGES (five things, nothing else)
#
#   1. Rename.  class SakkirinaSolo -> class SakkirinaScaled, so both can be
#      loaded as separate opponents in the same GameRunner process.
#
#   2. SOT_BASELINE_TIME_SCALE (default 1.0) applied to BOTH time constants:
#      the 9.8s TurnTimeout and the 0.65s per-move cap. Read once per game in a
#      new PregamePrepare override. At the default it is timing-identical to
#      stock.
#
#      BOTH, not one. scripts/sakkirina_half.patch measured what happens
#      otherwise: scaling only the 0.65s per-call cap and leaving TurnTimeout at
#      9.8s produced ~73% of stock's eval rate where ~45% was intended, because
#      cheaper individual calls simply meant more of them fit under the same
#      unscaled per-turn ceiling before usedTimeInTurn >= TurnTimeout forced a
#      fast move.
#
#      A set-but-unparseable or out-of-range value is REJECTED and logged, not
#      silently treated as 1.0 -- a run whose label says 0.2 while it quietly
#      played at stock speed would be indistinguishable from a real result.
#      Parsed with InvariantCulture, so "0.5" means the same thing on a
#      German-locale login node as it does here.
#
#   3. Evaluations-per-turn counter.  Evaluate() becomes non-static and
#      increments a counter; Play()'s turn-start block closes out the previous
#      turn; GameEnd emits a "SakkirinaScaled.EvalsPerTurn:" summary line.
#      Per TURN, not per second (swamped by opponent thinking time) and not per
#      game (swamped by game length) -- both of those move when the time budget
#      moves, which is the variable under study. Two agents in one game play the
#      same number of turns +/-1, so per-turn is what is comparable between
#      them. Logs via BotLog (defined in Bots/src/DeepSetsCore.cs), which writes
#      nothing unless SOT_LOG=1.
#
#   4. Null-move fix.  Same as scripts/sakkirina_gen.patch and
#      scripts/sakkirina_half.patch: in Play(), bestMove comes from a tree that
#      may have been built under a different determinization, so it can have no
#      isomorphic match in the current possibleMoves, leaving `move` null;
#      upstream then returns null and the engine scores it as an illegal move.
#      bestMove is not a safe substitute (it need not be legal in this state),
#      so fall back to possibleMoves[0], which always is.
#
#      This is load-bearing here, not housekeeping: the treatment
#      (DeepSetsBotExp, via DeepSetsBlendBot) already carries the fix, so
#      without it the control and the treatment would differ in whether they
#      forfeit games to illegal moves as well as in time budget -- and at the
#      2s budget, where the tree is shallowest, that is exactly when the bug
#      fires most.
#
#   5. ProceedTree try/catch, so a tree-reuse failure costs a cache miss rather
#      than the game. Defense in depth behind (4).
#
# APPLY
#   patch -o Bots/src/SakkirinaScaled.cs Bots/src/SakkirinaSolo.cs \
#         < scripts/sakkirina_scaled.patch
#   (done for you by scripts/fetch_baselines.sh)
#
